WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:10.339
Welcome back. This is Autistic Experience
at School. You’ll find the Session Three

2
00:00:10.339 --> 00:00:15.150
summary on page ten of your Participant
Guide. Bring back the two or three

3
00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:19.830
students you’ve been holding in mind, as
different people with different

4
00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:24.642
strengths, preferences and experiences.
We’ll begin with communication and

5
00:00:24.642 --> 00:00:29.849
connection, then follow the school day as
students experience it. We’ll consider

6
00:00:29.849 --> 00:00:34.726
capacity, masking and belonging, widen our
attention to Autistic staff, and

7
00:00:34.726 --> 00:00:39.867
audit one school process. Your map and
audit will become the starting point for

8
00:00:39.867 --> 00:00:44.217
Learning Design after lunch. The
leadership question is what school

9
00:00:44.217 --> 00:00:49.029
conditions shape each person’s day. A bell
schedule tells us when a lesson

10
00:00:49.029 --> 00:00:53.379
starts. It cannot tell us everything about
what it takes to arrive,

11
00:00:53.379 --> 00:00:58.059
communicate and participate. One student’s
experience may prompt a wider

12
00:00:58.059 --> 00:01:03.134
question, while remaining that person’s
experience. Keep observations separate

13
00:01:03.134 --> 00:01:08.077
from assumptions, leave identifying
details out, and stay curious about what

14
00:01:08.077 --> 00:01:09.921
we still need to understand.

15
00:01:11.441 --> 00:01:16.524
Let’s begin with communication. Speech is
one way of communicating, and we’ll be

16
00:01:16.525 --> 00:01:21.285
looking at a much wider picture: how
students receive information, how they

17
00:01:21.286 --> 00:01:26.498
share what matters to them, and the ways
we make both possible. The amount someone

18
00:01:26.499 --> 00:01:31.388
speaks doesn’t tell us how much they
understand. A student may need different

19
00:01:31.389 --> 00:01:36.665
ways to understand an instruction, answer
a question, ask for help, request a pause

20
00:01:36.666 --> 00:01:41.684
or say no. All of those messages matter.
Communication access belongs in school

21
00:01:41.685 --> 00:01:46.253
design and school systems. We’ll talk
about practical options, including

22
00:01:46.254 --> 00:01:50.629
augmentative and alternative
communication, or A A C. There isn’t one

23
00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:55.262
approach that suits every student.
Individual assessment and intervention

24
00:01:55.263 --> 00:02:00.088
questions belong with the student’s speech
pathologist or relevant team; our

25
00:02:00.089 --> 00:02:04.335
focus here is what educators can notice,
make available and change.

26
00:02:05.841 --> 00:02:11.063
Let’s start with a quick, quiet think.
Since you woke up this morning, how have

27
00:02:11.064 --> 00:02:16.488
you communicated? See if you can think of
three different ways. Perhaps you spoke,

28
00:02:16.489 --> 00:02:21.577
sent a message, pointed, showed someone a
photo, moved away, or took a moment

29
00:02:21.578 --> 00:02:26.466
before answering. Give yourself about
twenty seconds. Then, if you’re with

30
00:02:26.467 --> 00:02:31.555
colleagues, two or three people might
share one example. Sharing is optional.

31
00:02:31.556 --> 00:02:35.840
Pause the video now if you’d like that
thinking and sharing time.

32
00:02:43.841 --> 00:02:49.536
Let’s come back together. We move between
ways of communicating all day, often

33
00:02:49.537 --> 00:02:54.048
without noticing. Writing, facial
expression, gesture, sign, a

34
00:02:54.049 --> 00:02:59.965
communication device and speech are all
real communication. One method isn’t more

35
00:02:59.966 --> 00:03:05.735
valid because it is spoken. That gives us
something useful to notice at school.

36
00:03:05.736 --> 00:03:11.061
When we say, ‘Use your words,’ are we
overlooking a message a student has

37
00:03:11.062 --> 00:03:16.756
already communicated? A point, a look, or
moving closer or further away can be

38
00:03:16.757 --> 00:03:22.748
worth attending to. Our job is to listen
across the ways that person communicates,

39
00:03:22.749 --> 00:03:26.889
while checking what a message means rather
than assuming.

40
00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:33.475
There are two parts to keep distinct here:
understanding and expressing.

41
00:03:33.476 --> 00:03:39.052
Understanding is information coming in and
making sense. That might be a spoken

42
00:03:39.053 --> 00:03:44.271
instruction, a written sequence, a
demonstration, or a gesture in context.

43
00:03:44.272 --> 00:03:49.777
Expressing is a message going out: an
idea, a feeling, a question or a choice,

44
00:03:49.778 --> 00:03:55.353
through any communication method. These
don’t necessarily look the same for one

45
00:03:55.354 --> 00:04:00.930
person. Someone may speak at length and
still need support to understand spoken

46
00:04:00.931 --> 00:04:06.650
information. Someone else may understand
much more than they can readily express.

47
00:04:06.651 --> 00:04:12.441
Neither fluent speech nor limited speech
gives us the whole picture. Communication

48
00:04:12.442 --> 00:04:17.661
can also change with the person, the
place, the demands and how someone is

49
00:04:17.662 --> 00:04:23.237
feeling. A A C means augmentative and
alternative communication. It can include

50
00:04:23.238 --> 00:04:28.957
gesture, signs, a communication board or
book, typing, or a communication device.

51
00:04:28.958 --> 00:04:34.248
Some people use it alongside speech and
some instead of speech. Repeated or

52
00:04:34.249 --> 00:04:39.396
scripted words can carry meaning too.
Listen for the context and possible

53
00:04:39.397 --> 00:04:43.686
purpose before correcting the form. We
don’t need to assign a

54
00:04:43.687 --> 00:04:48.833
language-processing label before
respecting a student’s communication. If

55
00:04:48.834 --> 00:04:54.339
we need an individual assessment, we
involve the relevant team. For our school

56
00:04:54.340 --> 00:05:00.130
practice, the question is: what does this
environment help the student understand,

57
00:05:00.131 --> 00:05:05.421
and what ways does it give them to
respond? Look at routines, documents and

58
00:05:05.422 --> 00:05:10.497
expectations too. Where do they assume
that speaking is the usual way to

59
00:05:10.498 --> 00:05:11.355
participate?

60
00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:17.120
We’re going to hear Autistic people
describe communication in their own

61
00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:21.724
words. As you watch, notice what supports
communication. Choose one idea that

62
00:05:21.724 --> 00:05:26.267
stands out to you. Let the speakers’
experiences lead your thinking, without

63
00:05:26.267 --> 00:05:29.598
expecting one account to describe every
Autistic person.

64
00:11:02.080 --> 00:11:06.580
What stood out to you about what supported
communication? Take a moment to

65
00:11:06.581 --> 00:11:11.574
choose one idea. You might think about a
communication strength, something another

66
00:11:11.575 --> 00:11:15.828
person did that helped, or the difference
the environment or available

67
00:11:15.829 --> 00:11:20.452
communication method made. If you’re
watching with colleagues, invite one or

68
00:11:20.453 --> 00:11:23.720
two brief responses. Pause here for that
conversation.

69
00:11:31.720 --> 00:11:36.486
As we come back, hold onto that specific
idea. There is no single Autistic

70
00:11:36.487 --> 00:11:41.121
communication profile, so we need to
listen to what helps the particular

71
00:11:41.122 --> 00:11:46.084
person. One practical point to carry into
school is availability. A student’s

72
00:11:46.085 --> 00:11:50.850
communication system needs to be where
communication happens: in class, at

73
00:11:50.851 --> 00:11:55.748
break, during transitions and when plans
change. It cannot be limited to one

74
00:11:55.749 --> 00:12:00.775
activity or a therapy session. We support
the student’s ways of communicating,

75
00:12:00.776 --> 00:12:05.346
working with the student, their family and
the relevant team. We aren’t

76
00:12:05.347 --> 00:12:10.308
prescribing a system from a workshop
example. As leaders, ask how that access

77
00:12:10.309 --> 00:12:14.944
continues across staff, places and
routines. Who needs to understand the

78
00:12:14.945 --> 00:12:19.319
student’s communication, and how will
useful information reach them?

79
00:12:20.841 --> 00:12:26.055
Turn to page eleven of your Participant
Guide, or use a sheet of paper. Put one

80
00:12:26.056 --> 00:12:31.136
of the students you have in mind in the
centre. Around them are home, school,

81
00:12:31.137 --> 00:12:36.151
friends, community and supports. Who is
part of this student’s communication

82
00:12:36.152 --> 00:12:40.964
network? Add just one person or place now.
Use roles rather than names: a

83
00:12:40.965 --> 00:12:46.246
family member, a classmate, a teacher,
someone at a club. You can write, draw or

84
00:12:46.247 --> 00:12:51.261
use dot points. If it helps, ask a
colleague to scribe your ideas. You don’t

85
00:12:51.262 --> 00:12:56.543
need to fill every space. Networks can be
small or changing, and an empty box is

86
00:12:56.544 --> 00:13:01.958
fine. Give yourself about a minute and a
half. Pause the video while you make that

87
00:13:01.959 --> 00:13:02.960
first addition.

88
00:13:10.961 --> 00:13:15.783
Keep that map open. You can return to the
rest later. Different people and places

89
00:13:15.784 --> 00:13:20.244
may reveal different parts of a student’s
communication. A family member, a

90
00:13:20.245 --> 00:13:25.006
classroom teacher and a support worker may
each notice something valuable. We’re

91
00:13:25.007 --> 00:13:29.829
not deciding who understands the student
best. We’re recognising that the picture

92
00:13:29.830 --> 00:13:34.470
is shared. Where we know little about part
of that network, we have a question

93
00:13:34.471 --> 00:13:38.811
to explore with the student and family,
rather than a gap to fill with an

94
00:13:38.812 --> 00:13:39.473
assumption.

95
00:13:41.001 --> 00:13:45.997
Now let’s think about what should travel
between those people and places, so the

96
00:13:45.998 --> 00:13:50.614
student doesn’t have to start again with
every new person. We’ll use three

97
00:13:50.615 --> 00:13:55.169
colours. Purple is a communication
strength. Blue is something that helps

98
00:13:55.170 --> 00:13:59.849
communication. Orange is something you
would like others to know. Write one

99
00:13:59.850 --> 00:14:04.720
note, two, or all three. You can use one
of your students, without identifying

100
00:14:04.721 --> 00:14:09.527
them, or a made-up example. If you don’t
have coloured notes, label the three

101
00:14:09.528 --> 00:14:14.398
categories on paper. This is also on page
eleven. Allow about three minutes to

102
00:14:14.399 --> 00:14:19.395
write. Then, if you’re together, spend a
few minutes sharing. You can add a note

103
00:14:19.396 --> 00:14:24.455
to the board or read one out, and you can
keep your notes private. Leave any note

104
00:14:24.456 --> 00:14:29.199
with identifying details off the shared
board. Pause now for the writing and

105
00:14:29.200 --> 00:14:30.271
optional sharing.

106
00:14:38.280 --> 00:14:43.012
Let’s bring our attention back. Look
across the ideas available to you. What

107
00:14:43.013 --> 00:14:47.429
similarities do you notice? What strengths
might be missed if we looked

108
00:14:47.430 --> 00:14:52.478
only for speech? Which supports involve
changing what other people do or provide?

109
00:14:52.479 --> 00:14:56.769
Think about what would help a relief
teacher, a specialist teacher or

110
00:14:56.770 --> 00:15:01.754
playground staff. Useful knowledge can
travel between classrooms, the playground

111
00:15:01.755 --> 00:15:06.487
and home, with the student’s and family’s
perspectives included. It can make

112
00:15:06.488 --> 00:15:11.094
continuity possible when the people around
a student change. Keep the blue

113
00:15:11.095 --> 00:15:15.637
notes in view: the things that help
communication. We’re going to connect

114
00:15:15.638 --> 00:15:20.560
those ideas with three practical options.
Now add the leadership question. What

115
00:15:20.561 --> 00:15:25.356
system carries this information in your
school, and where does it rely on one

116
00:15:25.357 --> 00:15:30.278
person remembering? Student profiles,
handover notes and relief-day information

117
00:15:30.279 --> 00:15:35.137
can support continuity. Check how the
student’s and family’s knowledge reaches

118
00:15:35.138 --> 00:15:39.301
the people who need it through your
school’s established processes.

119
00:15:40.840 --> 00:15:46.485
The first option is clear communication.
Use familiar, specific words and give one

120
00:15:46.486 --> 00:15:51.852
idea or step at a time, in a form and
amount the person can access. That might

121
00:15:51.853 --> 00:15:57.288
mean making a direction shorter or making
the first step clearer. The second is

122
00:15:57.289 --> 00:16:01.749
processing time. After giving information,
pause. A person may be

123
00:16:01.750 --> 00:16:07.255
making sense of what you said, organising
a response and communicating it. If we

124
00:16:07.256 --> 00:16:12.762
fill every pause with a new version of the
question, we may add more information

125
00:16:12.763 --> 00:16:18.198
before the earlier information has been
processed. The third is visual support:

126
00:16:18.199 --> 00:16:23.913
showing information as well as saying it,
so there is something to return to. These

127
00:16:23.914 --> 00:16:29.350
are options to match to the person and the
moment, rather than a checklist that

128
00:16:29.351 --> 00:16:34.926
guarantees success. Keep the student’s A A
C available and accept their preferred

129
00:16:34.927 --> 00:16:40.084
ways of responding, including refusal. A
useful communication support makes

130
00:16:40.085 --> 00:16:45.730
messages easier to understand and share.
It doesn’t require the student to produce

131
00:16:45.731 --> 00:16:50.957
the answer or communication style we were
expecting. When these supports are

132
00:16:50.958 --> 00:16:56.254
expected across the school, a student’s
access is less dependent on one staff

133
00:16:56.255 --> 00:17:01.133
member. Leaders can ask where that
consistency already exists and where

134
00:17:01.134 --> 00:17:03.502
staff need information or support.

135
00:17:05.040 --> 00:17:10.203
Visual supports are information you can
see and come back to. The three examples

136
00:17:10.204 --> 00:17:14.975
here answer different questions. A timer
can show how much time is left. A

137
00:17:14.976 --> 00:17:20.008
sequence can show what to do next. A daily
schedule can show what is happening

138
00:17:20.009 --> 00:17:25.303
today. You may already use a calendar, a
shopping list or a reminder on your phone

139
00:17:25.304 --> 00:17:30.205
in much the same way. The format needs to
make sense to the person. Objects,

140
00:17:30.206 --> 00:17:35.043
photographs, written words and symbols can
all be options, on paper or on a

141
00:17:35.044 --> 00:17:39.095
device. Check that the student understands
what the information

142
00:17:39.096 --> 00:17:44.063
represents and can reach it when it is
needed. A support displayed across the

143
00:17:44.064 --> 00:17:49.097
room may exist without being useful at the
moment of need. Think of the visual

144
00:17:49.098 --> 00:17:54.261
information already available across your
school, including timetables, signage,

145
00:17:54.262 --> 00:17:59.032
assemblies and notices home. What question
does it answer? Could a student

146
00:17:59.033 --> 00:18:04.000
return to it without asking an adult? A
visual support offers information; it

147
00:18:04.001 --> 00:18:08.772
isn’t a condition the student must satisfy
to access something they enjoy.

148
00:18:08.773 --> 00:18:13.936
Visual supports and A A C can overlap, but
they aren’t interchangeable. Keep the

149
00:18:13.937 --> 00:18:19.165
person’s communication methods available
as well. We’ll return to this in Session

150
00:18:19.166 --> 00:18:22.826
Four when you choose a design option for a
school system.

151
00:18:24.360 --> 00:18:28.978
Double empathy asks us to look at
understanding in both directions. When

152
00:18:28.979 --> 00:18:33.922
people have different experiences and ways
of communicating, it can be harder

153
00:18:33.923 --> 00:18:38.151
for them to understand one another. That
doesn’t mean every social

154
00:18:38.152 --> 00:18:43.486
misunderstanding sits inside the Autistic
student. Consider a classroom discussion,

155
00:18:43.487 --> 00:18:48.170
group task or playground interaction.
Whose expectations are shaping what

156
00:18:48.171 --> 00:18:53.375
counts as joining in? Who is expected to
adjust? What would make this interaction

157
00:18:53.376 --> 00:18:58.384
clearer or safer for the people involved?
For example, an adult might expect a

158
00:18:58.385 --> 00:19:02.873
quick spoken response as a sign of
connection. A student might connect

159
00:19:02.874 --> 00:19:07.687
through a shared activity, through
detailed talk about an interest, or with

160
00:19:07.688 --> 00:19:12.241
more time between turns. Those are
possible differences to explore, not

161
00:19:12.242 --> 00:19:17.186
conclusions to impose on a particular
child. The question is how each student

162
00:19:17.187 --> 00:19:22.521
connects. Connection doesn’t need to look
one way. Our expectations and the context

163
00:19:22.522 --> 00:19:27.465
shape the interaction too, so we have
something useful to examine and change.

164
00:19:27.466 --> 00:19:32.605
We can make room for different ways of
being together while continuing to listen

165
00:19:32.606 --> 00:19:37.939
to what each person actually wants. Which
school practices favour one social style?

166
00:19:37.940 --> 00:19:42.819
Think about how groups are formed, what is
recognised at assemblies and what

167
00:19:42.820 --> 00:19:47.243
counts as participation. These are
expectations a leadership team can

168
00:19:47.244 --> 00:19:50.040
examine with the people who experience
them.

169
00:19:51.561 --> 00:19:57.125
Let’s carry that idea into the many ways
students connect. As we watch this next

170
00:19:57.126 --> 00:20:02.339
clip, keep the question open: how does
each student connect? Notice people,

171
00:20:02.340 --> 00:20:07.763
places, activities and ways of joining. A
different style of connection is not

172
00:20:07.764 --> 00:20:10.228
evidence that connection is absent.

173
00:20:55.321 --> 00:20:59.536
Keep those possibilities in mind as we
widen the picture beyond a single

174
00:20:59.537 --> 00:21:04.167
interaction. We can look for connection in
a student’s preferred activities and

175
00:21:04.168 --> 00:21:08.322
relationships, while allowing their own
ways of showing it to guide us.

176
00:21:09.841 --> 00:21:14.123
A student’s social world can include
family, school, community and online

177
00:21:14.124 --> 00:21:18.644
spaces. Connection might be one to one,
playing or working alongside someone,

178
00:21:18.645 --> 00:21:23.106
sharing a great deal about an interest, or
seeing someone only occasionally.

179
00:21:23.107 --> 00:21:27.806
Being together quietly can matter as much
as a lively conversation. Culture also

180
00:21:27.807 --> 00:21:32.386
shapes expectations about interaction and
relationships. We need to be careful

181
00:21:32.387 --> 00:21:36.788
about treating the pattern most familiar
to us as the measure of meaningful

182
00:21:36.789 --> 00:21:41.250
connection for everyone. There may be
spaces outside home and school where a

183
00:21:41.251 --> 00:21:45.712
student feels a particular sense of
belonging: a club, a community activity,

184
00:21:45.713 --> 00:21:50.352
or another shared-interest space. There
may be online connections too. We don’t

185
00:21:50.353 --> 00:21:54.278
need to disclose a student’s online
activity, identity or community

186
00:21:54.279 --> 00:21:58.502
membership to recognise that these
possibilities exist. Keep the picture

187
00:21:58.503 --> 00:22:03.023
individual. What do we know about this
student’s preferred people, places and

188
00:22:03.024 --> 00:22:07.425
ways of joining? What are we assuming?
What could we find out respectfully?

189
00:22:08.961 --> 00:22:13.896
Find a partner for a conversation about
connection. The questions are on page

190
00:22:13.897 --> 00:22:18.898
twelve of your Participant Guide. First,
when do the students you have in mind

191
00:22:18.899 --> 00:22:23.834
seem most comfortable connecting with
others at school? Second, what kinds of

192
00:22:23.835 --> 00:22:28.966
connection does each enjoy? That might be
talking, gaming, sport, making things,

193
00:22:28.967 --> 00:22:33.577
shared interests, or being together
quietly. Third, are there times when

194
00:22:33.578 --> 00:22:38.514
being social seems enjoyable, and times
when it seems tiring or overwhelming?

195
00:22:38.515 --> 00:22:43.450
Finally, what could educators change to
make connection feel safer, easier or

196
00:22:43.451 --> 00:22:48.451
more meaningful? Use about five minutes.
Keep identifying details private, and

197
00:22:48.452 --> 00:22:53.258
hold anything you don’t know as a
question. You can reflect individually if

198
00:22:53.259 --> 00:22:57.350
you’re watching on your own. Pause the
video for the discussion.

199
00:23:05.360 --> 00:23:10.283
Let’s come back to the last question: what
could educators change around the

200
00:23:10.284 --> 00:23:15.338
student? If you’re together, you might
offer one practical possibility without

201
00:23:15.339 --> 00:23:20.197
describing the student. Notice whether
your idea makes room for the kind of

202
00:23:20.198 --> 00:23:25.121
connection the student values. Does it
change an expectation, an activity, a

203
00:23:25.122 --> 00:23:30.439
space or the pace of interaction? Does it
leave room for choosing when to join and

204
00:23:30.440 --> 00:23:35.757
when to step back? The aim is meaningful
connection for that person. We don’t need

205
00:23:35.758 --> 00:23:40.812
to make everyone look social in the same
way. At school level, that could mean

206
00:23:40.813 --> 00:23:45.998
examining lunchtime clubs, quiet spaces at
break or how groups are formed. Treat

207
00:23:45.999 --> 00:23:51.119
each as a possibility to discuss with
students, rather than deciding in advance

208
00:23:51.120 --> 00:23:53.285
what connection should look like.

209
00:23:54.801 --> 00:24:00.438
Now we’ll look at daily capacity.
Communication and connection are part of

210
00:24:00.439 --> 00:24:05.690
a much larger day, and that day asks
different amounts of energy from

211
00:24:05.691 --> 00:24:11.406
different students. As we move into the
next activities, we’ll use a simple

212
00:24:11.407 --> 00:24:17.585
question: what costs, and what lightens?
That language will help us look beyond a

213
00:24:17.586 --> 00:24:23.609
single task to the pattern of demands and
opportunities for recovery across the

214
00:24:23.610 --> 00:24:23.917
day.

215
00:24:25.441 --> 00:24:30.237
Think of a student’s available energy as a
battery. Each day starts with some

216
00:24:30.238 --> 00:24:35.034
charge, and it isn’t necessarily the same
amount as yesterday. Activities and

217
00:24:35.035 --> 00:24:39.578
demands draw on that energy. As the
battery runs lower, managing the next

218
00:24:39.579 --> 00:24:44.312
demand can become harder. You may also
hear people in the Autistic community

219
00:24:44.313 --> 00:24:49.298
talk about spoons. The idea is having a
limited supply available: an action uses

220
00:24:49.299 --> 00:24:54.159
one spoon or several, and when spoons are
low, there is less left for the next

221
00:24:54.160 --> 00:24:58.955
thing. These are ways of talking about
capacity, rather than a measure we can

222
00:24:58.956 --> 00:25:03.689
calculate for someone else. The other part
of the picture is recharging. The

223
00:25:03.690 --> 00:25:08.676
right supports can reduce demand and help
restore some capacity. A pause, useful

224
00:25:08.677 --> 00:25:13.220
information, a preferred way to
communicate or a safe connection may make

225
00:25:13.221 --> 00:25:18.143
a difference, depending on the person. So
we’ll look for both sides in a school

226
00:25:18.144 --> 00:25:22.688
day: what draws on capacity, and what
helps to restore it. What helps one

227
00:25:22.689 --> 00:25:25.021
student may be different for another.

228
00:25:26.561 --> 00:25:31.549
Capacity fluctuates. Like all of us,
Autistic students can have easier days

229
00:25:31.550 --> 00:25:36.741
and harder days. Stress, energy, sensory
demands and what else is happening in

230
00:25:36.742 --> 00:25:42.067
life can change what is manageable. That
means the same student may do something

231
00:25:42.068 --> 00:25:47.461
one day and need more support with it the
next. Yesterday’s success doesn’t prove

232
00:25:47.462 --> 00:25:52.923
that today’s difficulty is a choice, and
finishing a task doesn’t tell us how much

233
00:25:52.924 --> 00:25:58.047
effort it required. Hold that in mind when
you look at the students you know.

234
00:25:58.048 --> 00:26:03.238
We can ask what is different today, what
demands have already accumulated, and

235
00:26:03.239 --> 00:26:08.430
what support is available now. We don’t
have to know every cause before making

236
00:26:08.431 --> 00:26:13.757
information clearer or allowing a useful
pause. We do need to keep checking with

237
00:26:13.758 --> 00:26:19.082
the student and the people who know them,
instead of treating capacity as fixed.

238
00:26:20.600 --> 00:26:26.056
Turn to page thirteen. Follow a typical
school day as your students experience

239
00:26:26.057 --> 00:26:31.655
it, from arrival to departure. Include
whole-school routines: bells, assemblies,

240
00:26:31.656 --> 00:26:36.899
breaks, relief days and changes. A primary
setting might include specialist

241
00:26:36.900 --> 00:26:42.215
lessons and the playground. A secondary
setting might involve moving between

242
00:26:42.216 --> 00:26:47.459
rooms and teachers each period. Mark R for
a routine or transition, C for a

243
00:26:47.460 --> 00:26:51.924
change, T for a communication opportunity,
and P for a moment to

244
00:26:51.925 --> 00:26:57.452
choose, pause or recover. Then consider
how the same moments affect each of the

245
00:26:57.453 --> 00:27:02.909
students you have in mind. Follow their
experience, rather than simply copying

246
00:27:02.910 --> 00:27:08.154
the official timetable. Keep unknowns as
questions. Now step back. Where do

247
00:27:08.155 --> 00:27:13.681
demands or requests for support seem to
cluster across the school: a particular

248
00:27:13.682 --> 00:27:19.280
period, day of the week, or point in the
term? Draw on patterns you already know

249
00:27:19.281 --> 00:27:23.816
in general. This is a prompt for examining
school design, not for

250
00:27:23.817 --> 00:27:29.131
monitoring or labelling individual
students. Allow about seven minutes. Keep

251
00:27:29.132 --> 00:27:34.801
identifying details out, leave room for
the next layer, and pause the video while

252
00:27:34.802 --> 00:27:35.368
you map.

253
00:27:43.400 --> 00:27:48.327
Let’s pause our drawing there. You don’t
need a polished or complete map. This is

254
00:27:48.328 --> 00:27:53.070
a working picture to extend, rather than a
judgement of a student or a school.

255
00:27:53.071 --> 00:27:57.751
Look briefly at the same point in the day
for your different students. It may

256
00:27:57.752 --> 00:28:02.740
involve different communication, sensory
or task demands for each of them. That is

257
00:28:02.741 --> 00:28:07.668
why we’re keeping several real students in
view. Keep the map beside you; it will

258
00:28:07.669 --> 00:28:12.595
travel with us into Session Four. Look at
any school-wide pattern you noted. What

259
00:28:12.596 --> 00:28:16.722
question does it raise about the bell
schedule, assemblies or relief

260
00:28:16.723 --> 00:28:21.096
arrangements? The pattern gives us
somewhere to investigate. It does not

261
00:28:21.097 --> 00:28:24.545
establish the cause of a particular
student’s experience.

262
00:28:26.081 --> 00:28:31.109
Now turn to page fourteen and return to
your day map. Use one mark or colour for

263
00:28:31.110 --> 00:28:35.183
what costs: what adds demand or
uncertainty. Use another for what

264
00:28:35.184 --> 00:28:40.276
lightens: what reduces demand, restores
capacity or brings joy. Think back to the

265
00:28:40.277 --> 00:28:44.923
battery image: what drains, and what might
recharge? Add questions where a

266
00:28:44.924 --> 00:28:48.870
student’s experience isn’t known.
Something can be enjoyable or

267
00:28:48.871 --> 00:28:53.708
successfully completed and still take
considerable effort. We can record what

268
00:28:53.709 --> 00:28:58.547
we observe, but we can’t read an internal
state from a single moment. Include

269
00:28:58.548 --> 00:29:03.067
strengths, joy, safe connection and
belonging. We want a picture of what

270
00:29:03.068 --> 00:29:07.650
sustains a student as well as what is
difficult. Give yourself about five

271
00:29:07.651 --> 00:29:11.405
minutes to add this layer, and pause the
video while you do.

272
00:29:19.440 --> 00:29:24.209
Come back to the pattern across the day.
Where do several demands occur close

273
00:29:24.210 --> 00:29:28.978
together? Where are the opportunities for
relief or recovery? What is already

274
00:29:28.979 --> 00:29:34.062
working well and worth protecting? Treat
these marks as prompts for inquiry, not a

275
00:29:34.063 --> 00:29:38.706
score. What costs or lightens can differ
between students, and for the same

276
00:29:38.707 --> 00:29:43.287
student on different days. Choose one
pattern you would like to understand

277
00:29:43.288 --> 00:29:48.308
better. That will help when we examine a
school process shortly. Ask where access

278
00:29:48.309 --> 00:29:52.952
depends on one person, and where the
school provides recovery opportunities

279
00:29:52.953 --> 00:29:57.784
students can use more dependably. What is
already built into the day, and what

280
00:29:57.785 --> 00:29:59.981
depends on a student having to ask?

281
00:30:01.520 --> 00:30:06.885
This school-day stack gives us another way
to see accumulation. The darker bars

282
00:30:06.886 --> 00:30:11.976
represent demand. The lighter bars
represent relief and recovery. They’re a

283
00:30:11.977 --> 00:30:17.411
visual cue, rather than quantities we can
assign to a child. A moment that looks

284
00:30:17.412 --> 00:30:22.777
small by itself may arrive after many
other demands. The last transition of the

285
00:30:22.778 --> 00:30:27.455
day sits within everything that came
before it. Equally, a supportive

286
00:30:27.456 --> 00:30:32.821
interaction or an opportunity to recover
belongs in the picture too. So when we

287
00:30:32.822 --> 00:30:37.981
notice a difficult moment, we can ask what
the day has already asked of this

288
00:30:37.982 --> 00:30:42.934
student and what support has been
available. Keep this qualitative. We’re

289
00:30:42.935 --> 00:30:48.163
looking for patterns and questions that
help us improve access, while keeping

290
00:30:48.164 --> 00:30:52.635
relief, recovery and joy visible alongside
cost. From a leadership

291
00:30:52.636 --> 00:30:57.863
position, consider what bell times,
assemblies and relief arrangements add to

292
00:30:57.864 --> 00:31:03.023
this pattern. Where does the school day or
week offer relief? A whole-school

293
00:31:03.024 --> 00:31:08.113
routine can affect many students at once,
while affecting each differently.

294
00:31:09.641 --> 00:31:14.130
There are other things that can draw on
our batteries or spoons, including effort

295
00:31:14.131 --> 00:31:18.564
that isn’t obvious to someone watching.
Let’s hear the next video about masking.

296
00:31:18.565 --> 00:31:22.997
Keep in mind the difference between how a
person appears on the outside and what

297
00:31:22.998 --> 00:31:24.175
they may be managing.

298
00:32:54.601 --> 00:33:00.188
As we return from the video, keep that
distinction open. Visible behaviour gives

299
00:33:00.189 --> 00:33:05.352
us part of the picture. We’ll now connect
this with identity, capacity and

300
00:33:05.353 --> 00:33:06.767
belonging at school.

301
00:33:08.280 --> 00:33:13.952
A student can appear fine without feeling
fine. Quietness, compliance or completed

302
00:33:13.953 --> 00:33:19.414
work doesn’t give us a full account of
what someone is managing. Masking brings

303
00:33:19.415 --> 00:33:24.317
that question into our thinking about
identity and belonging. Hold four

304
00:33:24.318 --> 00:33:29.219
questions in mind. What might this student
be working hard to manage or

305
00:33:29.220 --> 00:33:34.751
hide? Where can they be recognised and
included? What supports belonging without

306
00:33:34.752 --> 00:33:40.284
requiring them to appear less Autistic?
And what does the student want others to

307
00:33:40.285 --> 00:33:45.186
understand? We can’t answer those
questions simply by watching from the

308
00:33:45.187 --> 00:33:50.299
outside. They invite us to listen and to
examine the conditions around the

309
00:33:50.300 --> 00:33:55.551
student. Include what feels safe and
joyful, the connections they value, and

310
00:33:55.552 --> 00:34:01.294
places where they can be themselves. This
is a reason to look beyond whether a task

311
00:34:01.295 --> 00:34:06.266
was finished or a lesson was quiet. Access
matters, and so do the effort

312
00:34:06.267 --> 00:34:11.309
involved and the student’s experience of
belonging. Leaders cannot know a

313
00:34:11.310 --> 00:34:16.701
student’s experience by watching from an
office, but they can shape conditions

314
00:34:16.702 --> 00:34:22.304
where different ways of participating are
accepted. Ask what the school can do to

315
00:34:22.305 --> 00:34:27.696
reduce the need to hide difficulty. If
this reflection feels personal, you are

316
00:34:27.697 --> 00:34:31.128
welcome to step out and return when you
are ready.

317
00:34:32.641 --> 00:34:37.614
Return to page twelve for the masking
discussion. With a partner, consider

318
00:34:37.615 --> 00:34:42.997
three questions. When might one of the
students you have in mind be working hard

319
00:34:42.998 --> 00:34:48.107
to appear fine at school when they are
not? When and where do they seem most

320
00:34:48.108 --> 00:34:53.012
comfortable being themselves? What could
we change to help them feel more

321
00:34:53.013 --> 00:34:58.395
comfortable and supported? You have about
four minutes. Keep examples anonymised

322
00:34:58.396 --> 00:35:03.710
and distinguish what you know from what
you wonder. Avoid assuming that a quiet

323
00:35:03.711 --> 00:35:08.479
student is coping, or that outward
behaviour tells you the whole story.

324
00:35:08.480 --> 00:35:10.999
Pause the video for the conversation.

325
00:35:19.001 --> 00:35:24.598
Let’s come back together. Focus on one
possible change, rather than a conclusion

326
00:35:24.599 --> 00:35:30.338
about what a student must be feeling. If
you’re sharing with colleagues, offer the

327
00:35:30.339 --> 00:35:35.865
change without identifying the child.
Perhaps your next step is to ask a better

328
00:35:35.866 --> 00:35:41.109
question, understand an existing source of
comfort, or change a demand in a

329
00:35:41.110 --> 00:35:46.707
routine. Whatever you choose, keep the
student’s perspective central. The aim is

330
00:35:46.708 --> 00:35:52.305
to make being themselves safer and better
supported, and to find out whether the

331
00:35:52.306 --> 00:35:53.226
change helps.

332
00:35:54.760 --> 00:35:59.572
Let’s widen the picture to Autistic staff.
The thinking we have used about

333
00:35:59.573 --> 00:36:04.649
sensory load, masking, communication and
energy also matters in staffrooms and

334
00:36:04.650 --> 00:36:09.397
meetings. Your school may include Autistic
staff who have disclosed that,

335
00:36:09.398 --> 00:36:14.540
and staff who have not. Their access and
flourishing matter in their own right.

336
00:36:14.541 --> 00:36:19.749
The strengths on the slide include deep
knowledge, precision and connection with

337
00:36:19.750 --> 00:36:24.034
neurodivergent students. These are
possibilities to recognise, not

338
00:36:24.035 --> 00:36:28.848
assumptions to assign to a person.
Strengths and support needs can both be

339
00:36:28.849 --> 00:36:34.123
present. Consider predictable meetings,
clear communication, choices about how to

340
00:36:34.124 --> 00:36:39.200
contribute and access to quiet space. Many
of these practices can be available

341
00:36:39.201 --> 00:36:43.551
without anyone needing to disclose
personal information. Individual

342
00:36:43.552 --> 00:36:48.628
workplace adjustments also follow the
processes in your school or system. Take

343
00:36:48.629 --> 00:36:53.310
about two minutes privately. Choose an
ordinary staff process, such as a

344
00:36:53.311 --> 00:36:57.859
meeting, the way changes are communicated,
or how relief is allocated.

345
00:36:57.860 --> 00:37:03.068
What is already clear and predictable?
Where is flexibility possible? What would

346
00:37:03.069 --> 00:37:07.882
someone have to ask for, or disclose, to
get what they need? Who should be

347
00:37:07.883 --> 00:37:12.959
involved before changing that process?
Keep this reflection about the process.

348
00:37:12.960 --> 00:37:18.102
Please do not identify or speculate about
colleagues. Pause the video while you

349
00:37:18.103 --> 00:37:18.629
reflect.

350
00:37:26.640 --> 00:37:31.255
Keep that reflection with you. You can
carry a question about the process into

351
00:37:31.256 --> 00:37:35.991
the design work without sharing anyone’s
personal circumstances. A culture where

352
00:37:35.992 --> 00:37:40.007
people can be open begins with what
happens in everyday routines and

353
00:37:40.008 --> 00:37:44.323
conversations. Build that safety, and
never ask anyone to disclose. We’ll

354
00:37:44.324 --> 00:37:48.280
return to the part leaders play in shaping
culture in Session Four.

355
00:37:49.800 --> 00:37:55.271
Turn to page fifteen for Audit at school
level. Choose one process: enrolment and

356
00:37:55.272 --> 00:38:00.263
starting at the school, assemblies,
relief-day arrangements, how change is

357
00:38:00.264 --> 00:38:05.530
communicated, or where students can go to
regulate. Work on your own or with a

358
00:38:05.531 --> 00:38:10.728
colleague. First, name the process. Follow
what a student encounters from the

359
00:38:10.729 --> 00:38:15.926
first point of contact through to the next
handover or outcome. Second, write

360
00:38:15.927 --> 00:38:20.714
what educators can observe without
interpretation. Third, list possible

361
00:38:20.715 --> 00:38:25.843
contributors in the environment or task,
including timing, communication and

362
00:38:25.844 --> 00:38:30.494
expectations. Fourth, record what lightens
demand and one question to

363
00:38:30.495 --> 00:38:35.897
investigate. Where is access built into
the process? Where does it depend on one

364
00:38:35.898 --> 00:38:40.753
person remembering, or on a student
asking? For example, in a relief-day

365
00:38:40.754 --> 00:38:46.225
process, you might ask who tells students
about the change and whether the relief

366
00:38:46.226 --> 00:38:51.286
teacher receives useful information. In a
regulation-space process, ask how

367
00:38:51.287 --> 00:38:56.074
students reach it and when it is
available. Those are examples to guide

368
00:38:56.075 --> 00:39:01.409
your questions, not findings about your
school. Allow about seven minutes. Keep

369
00:39:01.410 --> 00:39:06.675
the audit reflective and anonymised. We
are examining the design of a process,

370
00:39:06.676 --> 00:39:12.078
not ranking the school or evaluating a
named staff member. Pause the video while

371
00:39:12.079 --> 00:39:12.694
you work.

372
00:39:20.720 --> 00:39:26.190
Let’s bring that audit to a close. What
did it confirm, and what surprised you?

373
00:39:26.191 --> 00:39:31.450
If you share, offer a process question
rather than an identifiable incident.

374
00:39:31.451 --> 00:39:36.079
Check that you have separated observations
from interpretations and

375
00:39:36.080 --> 00:39:41.620
included what already helps. Where does
access depend on one person? Where could

376
00:39:41.621 --> 00:39:46.740
the process make it more dependable? These
questions may cross portfolios,

377
00:39:46.741 --> 00:39:51.720
including the front office, daily
organisation and the executive. Choose

378
00:39:51.721 --> 00:39:57.191
one routine or process to carry into the
design work, and keep both the map and

379
00:39:57.192 --> 00:39:58.242
audit with you.

380
00:39:59.760 --> 00:40:05.085
On page sixteen, or your Carry into design
card, choose a clear focus for the

381
00:40:05.086 --> 00:40:10.340
next session. For Keep, name one school
practice worth protecting because it

382
00:40:10.341 --> 00:40:15.806
supports access, connection or joy. For
Reduce, name one barrier or unnecessary

383
00:40:15.807 --> 00:40:20.992
demand. Then tick the category that best
fits your Reduce item: culture and

384
00:40:20.993 --> 00:40:26.108
learning, routines, or teams and
resourcing. Those categories will help us

385
00:40:26.109 --> 00:40:31.644
form trios in Session Four. For Explore,
write one question to take to students,

386
00:40:31.645 --> 00:40:37.390
families or staff. Keep the question open
if their knowledge is still needed. Allow

387
00:40:37.391 --> 00:40:42.856
about three minutes. Your responses can
stay private. Pause the video to finish

388
00:40:42.857 --> 00:40:46.850
the card, then keep it with your
school-day map and audit.

389
00:40:54.880 --> 00:40:59.636
Keep students first as you look at those
three choices. Design should protect what

390
00:40:59.637 --> 00:41:04.099
supports access, connection and joy as
well as reduce a barrier. Consultation

391
00:41:04.100 --> 00:41:08.621
can help us understand the questions still
open. We’ll take the map, the audit

392
00:41:08.622 --> 00:41:13.142
and your Reduce item into the learning
design session after lunch. In the live

393
00:41:13.143 --> 00:41:17.782
workshop, lunch runs from twelve thirty to
one. For this recording, you can take

394
00:41:17.783 --> 00:41:22.069
a break between sessions and return when
you’re ready. Before we close the

395
00:41:22.070 --> 00:41:26.355
morning, let’s come back to the fuller
picture of the students you’ve been

396
00:41:26.356 --> 00:41:27.295
holding in mind.

397
00:41:28.800 --> 00:41:33.128
Across Sessions Two and Three, you’ve been
building a picture of those

398
00:41:33.129 --> 00:41:37.958
students. How the world arrives through
their senses. How each thinks, focuses

399
00:41:37.959 --> 00:41:43.103
and builds understanding. Their strengths
and interests. Their internal experience.

400
00:41:43.104 --> 00:41:47.996
How they reach towards other people, and
what that can cost when the conditions

401
00:41:47.997 --> 00:41:52.764
don’t support them. Managing a school day
can involve sensory demands, social

402
00:41:52.765 --> 00:41:57.093
interpretation, executive effort and
communication across differences.

403
00:41:57.094 --> 00:42:01.672
Sometimes it also involves working to
appear fine when fine is not what is

404
00:42:01.673 --> 00:42:06.629
happening. That effort uses resources. A
child who knows who they are, and feels

405
00:42:06.630 --> 00:42:10.706
valued for it, has more of those resources
available for learning,

406
00:42:10.707 --> 00:42:15.035
connection and self-knowledge. The
research discussed in this workshop

407
00:42:15.036 --> 00:42:19.740
connects a positive sense of Autistic
identity with better mental health and

408
00:42:19.741 --> 00:42:24.446
wellbeing. Belonging matters. So does
agency: meaningful choice about how to

409
00:42:24.447 --> 00:42:29.151
communicate, where to be, and when to step
back. Knowing that other Autistic

410
00:42:29.152 --> 00:42:33.730
people share your experience can matter
too. There are Autistic adults and

411
00:42:33.731 --> 00:42:38.310
communities in which people find belonging
and purpose. These students are

412
00:42:38.311 --> 00:42:42.827
not alone. This belongs within our
educational work. We can make room for

413
00:42:42.828 --> 00:42:46.779
identity, connection and
self-understanding in the conditions we

414
00:42:46.780 --> 00:42:51.547
create every day. For leaders, belonging,
agency and connection belong in the

415
00:42:51.548 --> 00:42:56.253
school’s culture and routines, and in the
way staff are supported. Take that

416
00:42:56.254 --> 00:43:01.020
thought with you into the break. The
